# STATUS.md is the record of open work, and it travels with the code (all AIs, all models)

The owner works on two machines. They kept redoing work that was already finished, because the record of
what was open lived in one assistant's memory instead of in the repository. **`STATUS.md` at the repo root
is the only record of what work is open.** Not your memory, not a chat, not `MEMORY.md`, not a scratch file
under `.superpowers/` (that folder is git-ignored and exists on one machine only).

## Before you say anything about what is pending

- Open `STATUS.md` and read it. Never answer "I don't know what was left open".
- An entry may be stale. Re-check it against the real code before you believe it or act on it.
- `docs/STATUS_CLOSED.md` holds finished work. **Do not propose anything listed there again.**

## Update it in the same commit as the work it describes

Never leave `STATUS.md` for "a later commit". A later commit is the one that does not happen.

The shape (the three sections, in this order):

- `## ส่งต่อรอบหน้า` — work handed to the next session. Each item needs four fields: a bold subject,
  `**ให้ทำอะไร:**`, `**ที่มา:**`, `**หลักฐาน:**`. Do not touch the blockquote lines under this heading.
- `## เปิดอยู่` — work still open. An item is a column-one `- ` bullet and needs at least one of
  `· ตรวจ: <id>` (something a script or a person can check) or `· รอเจ้าของ: <what the owner must decide>`.
  At most one line of each kind per item.
- `## ปิดแล้ว (สารบัญ)` — one line per finished topic, written `- **YYYY-MM-DD** — <title>`.

Every non-blank line inside those sections is a column-one `- ` item, an indented continuation, or a `>`
blockquote. The file has hard size limits (150 lines, 20 KB).

**`tests/status-structure.test.js` is the authority, not this summary.** It enforces all of the above and
more, and its error messages say what to fix. Run `npm run check` before you finish, and do not finish
while it is red. Never loosen the test to make a `STATUS.md` edit pass.

## When you finish a piece of work

1. Put a `Closes:` trailer in the commit message naming the item you closed (its `· ตรวจ:` id when it has
   one, otherwise its bold title).
2. Remove the item from `## เปิดอยู่`.
3. Append the record of what was done, what was verified and who decided to `docs/STATUS_CLOSED.md`, under
   the `## ปิดไปแล้ว <date>` section whose date **is the date you are closing on** — and create that section
   if it does not exist yet. Do not append under the newest section when its date is an earlier day: the
   index line you add in step 4 carries the date of the heading its record sits under, and the test compares
   the two. A brand-new section needs nothing extra: the index line from step 4 is what keeps it covered by
   the both-directions rule. **Never edit the content already in that file** — it was copied verbatim on
   purpose so that it works as evidence. Append only.
4. Add the matching line to `## ปิดแล้ว (สารบัญ)`, written `- **YYYY-MM-DD** — <title>`. Its title must be
   copied exactly from the bold title (or heading) of the record you just appended, and its date must be the
   date of the `## ` heading that record sits under. Never backdate it to match an older heading — move the
   record under the right heading instead.
5. Never record something as done that you could not verify. Say plainly what was and was not checked.

## Never edit `Z:\Time_Attendance` directly

`Z:\Time_Attendance` is the **live website**. Open Cursor on a worktree (an ordinary local folder, for example
`C:\Users\<you>\ta-work`), make changes on a branch there, and let them reach the live site only through
the finishing steps in section 9 of `AGENTS.md`. Editing the live folder by hand is how production gets
broken with no history to roll back to. If you think you need to write there, stop and ask the human.

Read `AGENTS.md` before touching anything else.
